  @section what-is-rodin What is Rodin?

  Rodin is a lightweight and modular C++20 finite element framework designed
  for solving partial differential equations (PDEs) and performing shape and
  topology optimization.

  It is named after the French sculptor [Auguste
  Rodin](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Auguste_Rodin), considered the founder
  of modern sculpture.

  @m_class{m-block m-success m-badge}

  @parblock
  <img src="Images/Maintainer.png" alt="Carlos Brito-Pacheco's GitHub profile picture" />
  <h2>Active development</h2>

  The library is in active development and primarily maintained by
  [Carlos Brito-Pacheco](https://cbritopacheco.github.io/), who is actively looking for
  co-maintainers.
  @endparblock

  @section poisson-example-mainpage A simple example

  The library provides a compositional form language that lets
  you express variational problems in C++ code that closely mirrors the
  mathematical notation. For example, the Poisson problem with homogeneous Dirichlet boundary
  conditions, in strong form, for a domain @f$\Omega@f$ with boundary
  @f$\partial\Omega@f$ is

  @f[
    \begin{aligned}
      -\Delta u &= f && \text{in } \Omega,\\
      u &= 0 && \text{on } \partial\Omega.
    \end{aligned}
  @f]

  The corresponding weak form is: find @f$u \in H^1_0(\Omega)@f$ such that

  @f[
    \int_\Omega \nabla u \cdot \nabla v \, dx
      = \int_\Omega f v \, dx
      \qquad \forall v \in H^1_0(\Omega).
  @f]

  In the code one can implement it as such:

  @code{.cpp}
  Mesh mesh;
  mesh = mesh.UniformGrid(Polytope::Type::Triangle, {16, 16});
  mesh.getConnectivity().compute(1, 2);

  P1 vh(mesh);
  TrialFunction u(vh);
  TestFunction  v(vh);

  Problem problem(u, v);
  problem = Integral(Grad(u), Grad(v))
          - Integral(f, v)
          + DirichletBC(u, Zero());

  Solver::CG(problem).solve();
  @endcode

  @section rodin-architecture Architecture

  The core modules form the central stack. Models build on these interfaces
  to the left; third-party integrations and dependencies sit to the right.
  Shared services support the library across these layers.

  @image html Images/Architecture.svg width=100%

  @section download-and-build Download and build

  See the @ref guides-getting-started-installation "Installation guide" for
  detailed instructions, dependency lists, and platform-specific notes.

  @m_class{m-block m-success}

  @parblock
  <h2>Bash installation</h2>

  @code{.bash}
  git clone --recursive https://github.com/cbritopacheco/rodin
  cd rodin
  git lfs install
  git lfs pull  # required for resources
  mkdir build && cd build
  cmake .. -DCMAKE_BUILD_TYPE=Release
  make -j4
  @endcode
  @endparblock

  @m_class{m-block m-info}

  @parblock
  <h2>Git LFS</h2>

  If you only need the library and headers, configure with
  `-DRODIN_INSTALL_RESOURCES=OFF` and skip hydrating the Git LFS resources.
  @endparblock

  @section where-to-start Where to start?

  <strong>New to Rodin? Start by solving a complete problem.</strong> Follow the
  steps below to install the library, build a first program, solve the Poisson
  equation, and visualize the result.

  @m_class{m-block m-default}

  @parblock
  <h2>1. Install Rodin</h2>

  Set up the dependencies and build the library using the
  @ref guides-getting-started-installation "Installation guide".
  @endparblock

  @m_class{m-block m-default}

  @parblock
  <h2>2. Build your first program</h2>

  Follow @ref guides-getting-started-first-steps "First Steps" to learn how
  meshes, finite element spaces, trial and test functions, and solvers fit together.
  @endparblock

  @m_class{m-block m-default}

  @parblock
  <h2>3. Solve and visualize a problem</h2>

  Work through @ref guides-getting-started-first-problem "Your First Problem"
  to solve the Poisson equation, then use the
  @ref guides-io "I/O and visualization guide" to export the solution for
  inspection in ParaView.
  @endparblock

  For examples of results produced with Rodin, visit the @ref gallery "Gallery".

  @section rodin-philosophy Philosophy behind Rodin

  The architecture follows [mosra](https://mosra.cz/)'s
  ["batteries included, but screws not glued in"](https://magnum.graphics/)
  philosophy from the Magnum Engine project. For Rodin it means meshes, finite
  element spaces, forms, assembly, and solvers work together, while their
  interfaces remain accessible for extensions. A custom solver, constitutive
  law, or integrator can reuse the surrounding components.

  The form language keeps the mathematics visible in C++. For real-valued
  scalar fields @f$u@f$, @f$v@f$, and @f$f@f$ on @f$\Omega@f$,
  @f[
    \begin{aligned}
      \texttt{Integral(Grad(u), Grad(v))}
        &\quad\longleftrightarrow\quad
        \int_\Omega \nabla u \cdot \nabla v \, dx, \\
      \texttt{Integral(f, v)}
        &\quad\longleftrightarrow\quad
        \int_\Omega f v \, dx.
    \end{aligned}
  @f]
  Spaces, integrals, and boundary conditions are composed explicitly; assembly
  and the chosen solver realize the discrete problem. Module boundaries follow
  these responsibilities, allowing new models to reuse existing geometry,
  evaluation, and algebra.

